From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 09:10:20 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] inkobj: Implement ink, strokes, the renderer and their
 helpers.

PowerPoint's slide show pen creates an InkRenderer and gives up at
once when it is not there, as it was not: inkobj was an empty stub.
Word, Excel and the shared Office modules use InkDisp too.  This
implements the classes Office refers to, with the SDK's layouts and
the type library for IDispatch:

InkDisp holds strokes made from packet data, X and Y first, in
HIMETRIC; each has an id from 1 up, drawing attributes and extended
properties.  Strokes can be read back, moved, scaled, rotated, sheared,
transformed, split, hit-tested and bounded; collections of them and
named collections of them can be made, and strokes can be deleted,
extracted, copied and cloned with the ink.  A deleted stroke says so.

InkRenderer draws strokes through the object transform, the view
transform and the device's resolution, with GDI+ for the copy-pen
operation (antialiasing, transparency, ball or rectangle tip, a
cardinal spline for curve fitting) and with GDI's ROP2 for the others;
it converts between pixels and ink space and measures strokes.

InkDrawingAttributes has the documented defaults: a black ball pen 53
wide, 1 high, antialiased, opaque, copying.  InkTransform and
InkRectangle are GDI's XFORM and RECT with the SDK's operations.

Only these five classes are registered; the others of the type library
(InkCollector, InkOverlay, the recognizers) stay unregistered, as they
are not implemented.  Not done: ISF saving and loading, clipboard
transfer, clipping to a rectangle, pressure changing the pen's width.

Not measured on Windows yet: the order InkTransform composes in (a new
operation applies after the transform there is), how bounds are rounded
and how far the pen widens them, what a deleted stroke's methods
answer, whether a stroke keeps the attributes object it is given, and
how pixels and ink space round.  The tests print the first two.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

